**Proposition 10.2 (exact body, wrapper and channel subaccounts).** Write
$\ell(w)=|w|$. The exact length functional is

$$
\ell(U(n))=n+1,\quad \ell(F(w))=2\ell(w)+1,\quad
\ell(\mathcal L(w_1,\ldots,w_t))=1+\sum_i(2\ell(w_i)+2).
$$

Together with Definitions 5.3/6.1 this specifies exact M/T body lengths,
including every repeated record and unary reference. For example a memory
cell entry has length $27+2(\mathrm{port}+\mathrm{position})$. A new full
View wrapper at stage $j$ has length

$$
L_j=29+2\bigl(j+\ell(\mathrm{serviceBinding})+\ell(\xi)
                     +\ell(b)+\ell(v_j)\bigr).
$$

On one successful flight let $L_C,L_P,L_I,L_0,\ldots,L_4$ be the actual
eight full publication lengths. Their disjoint two-copy-plus-print body
subtotal, ending after the second handoff and mandatory print, is

$$
\sum_X(17L_X+7)=17\sum_X L_X+56.
$$

The full flight count is this subtotal plus exactly the sum of all other
actual expanded rows of Definition 10.1. That remainder includes setup,
exports, allocation, positioning, parsing, decoding, checks, ACKs and return;
it is not a constant overhead. Distinct sender/mailbox/receiver payloads
alone retain at least $3\sum_X L_X$ ordinary cells.

Proof. Each Frame adds its unary length/zero and payload; each list item
adds its tag/Frame, giving the functional. Apply it to the displayed cell
tuple and seven-field wrapper, with $\ell(U(j))=j+1$, four-bit kind and
two-bit view tag, to obtain the counts. Each publication retains ARC's
$12L+6$ two-copy/second-handoff body and $5L+1$ print body. The eight
protected segments are disjoint actual intervals. Definition 10.1 counts
all remaining leaves at their actual shared successors. The three payload
copies occupy distinct immutable intervals, so their storage lower bound
is additive. Delimiters/descriptors/banks/meter are additional. ∎

